A||||13||Eddie||? about CARPET||||5||
Z||000000||Eddie||12-15-1999||08:55 PM||||HAS ANY ONE REPLACED THE CARPET WITH THE CARPET FROM  jCwHITNEY IF SO ARE YOU HAPPY WITH IT!!! EDDIE||||reg||5
Z||000001||BENGAL4||12-15-1999||11:32 PM||||I don't know about J.C. Whitney's, but don't buy one through the Fiero Store. For $150.00, I got a gray carpet that looks like a jade green and the fit is terrible. Wished I would have purchased Mr. Mike's. Bought his seat covers and they are great.||||reg||13
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||12:56 AM||||Eddie--Your "caps lock" is on.  I'd like to know the same thing.  I need to replace my carpeting, but I'd like to spend less than $140 if possible.  Any other suppliers other than the above mentioned?||||reg||1
Z||000003||87FieroGT||12-16-1999||01:56 AM||||I think that Mr Mikes would be a good place to get it from. One thing to keep in mind though is that you might want to keep the old carpet in and lay the new stuff on top of it. I have heard that this is wise because the after market carpets do not have the same thick backing as the factory issued carpet. Good luck in finding the carpet.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||03:22 AM||||I've never replaced the carpet in a Fiero before, but I would think that it would be better to replace the padding instead of keeping the old carpet.  The car is small enough that I don't think that the new carpet would fit over the old.  Take off your shifter cover sometime and look at the top of the carpet to see one of the areas I'm talking about.  There is an indentation in the carpet there so it fits under the center console.  Also, there are 6(?) clips that hold the carpet in place.  I don't thing they are wide enough for 2 layers.  The deadpedal area is another, although I'm not 100% about that.  I don't think that padding could be very expensive for something as small as our cars.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||12-16-1999||04:18 AM||||Looking through a JC Whitney catalog right now. you can get underlay for cars.. 36" x 72" for $16. Can also get combination heat & noise insulation in a 48" x 72" roll for $25.<P>I'd assume either one or a combination of these 2 products (or similar) would be a suitable replacement for the factory padding.<BR>I think you'd lose some of the form from the factory padding, but it would be possible to build up areas where there is a raised area in the original padding. Besides asthetics, this could be important for areas where the carpet contacts other panels such as the center console.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||12-16-1999||10:05 AM||||I bought the JC Whitney carpet and I have to say I don't think it's the best deal out there.  When they say it's "molded" carpet what they mean is it's sewn to fit, so there are seams in the footwell area.  I got mine in black so the seams aren't that noticable, but if you wanted gray or tan, I think it would look terrible.  The fit is okay.  I just installed it over the original carpet.  If you've ever taken it out you would see that the stock carpet has molded foam all over the bottom and 1/8" rubber mat in between.  I'm sure it would make a better fit if you could lose the original carpet, but I think it would be a lot of work.||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||12-16-1999||06:59 PM||||Oddly enough most replacement carpetings are designed to go directly over the existing carpeting.  This is even the way that the famed Mr. Mike recomends.  <P>Monkey,<P>That is a good point about the clips.  I have taken them off several times during my stereo install.  Sometimes they are so much trouble, I don't even put the carpet back underneath.<P>||||reg||10
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||01:18 AM||||William--I can't blame you.  I took my clips out once and never put them back in.  If the new carpet is supposed to go over the old, wouldn't you think there'd be a clearance problem for other things, too.  What about the mouldings around the door or the console?  Would it affect the seat sliders?  I need to check this out.  I might give Mr. Mike a call in the am and see what he says to all of this.||||reg||1
Z||000009||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||06:17 PM||||Mr. Mike has an excellent FAQ page on his website.  He specifically states that you are best to cover the old carpet with the new.  I think that the replacement stuff is thinner.  Considering how forgiving plastic is, I would say that you won't have any problems tucking it below everything other than those @#$% clips.  Of course you realize that EVERYTHING that touches the carpet needs to come out.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric||12-17-1999||06:52 PM||||It's really not too bad.  I've had my interior apart a lot and it goes back together quite well (less some screws and clips).  I get a picture for you guys one of these days.  I painted all the light gray pieces black and left all the dark gray as is.  <P>If Mr. Mikes makes the carpet molded without seams, I'd go with them.  I think it costs $130, but the fit would look a lot better.  You should also check into matching carpet.  It's really easy to replace the dor carpet yourself, you wouldn't want to waste money on his kits.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||10:27 PM||||I tried to get a hold of Mr. Mike today, but all I got was his answering machine.  I'll have to try again on Monday.  His FAQ page brought up an interesting point, though.  Apparently the factory carpet is attached to the plastic liner.  I thought it was a separate piece.  Makes more sense now to lay the new over the old.||||reg||1
Z||000012||TRL46202||12-19-1999||02:16 AM||||Who is Mr. Mike and does he have a web page?<P>thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||12-19-1999||03:03 AM||||Mr. Mike sells some leather aftermarket interior parts (seats, lower door panel covers, carpet, etc).  His website is:  <A HREF="http://www.mrmikes.com" TARGET=_blank>www.mrmikes.com</A>   ||||reg||1
